## Decisions

**Brain file steps reuse the "brain" category.** The spec says content
template steps use `category: "brain"`. The alternative — adding a new
category like "brain-content" — would require updating the InitCategory
type union, the agent's category walk logic, and every test that enumerates
categories. Instead, applySteps uses `path.extname` on the filePath to
distinguish file steps (has extension) from directory steps (no extension).
This is a minimal, correct discriminant because brain directory steps point
to a directory path and brain file steps point to a `.md` file.

**applySteps update is step 1 (before provisionBrain changes).** The new
provisionBrain steps would be un-applyable without the applySteps update.
Sequencing it first means the apply layer is ready before the planning
layer produces new step shapes. This follows the dependency-order rule.

**Per-file idempotency over per-vault idempotency.** The spec explicitly
calls this out: each content file gets its own existence check. The
alternative — gating all content steps on the vault-level check — would
mean a vault that exists but lost its playbook hub cannot be repaired
without deleting the entire vault. The per-file approach costs three
`access()` calls but enables surgical re-provisioning.

**Templates as string constants, not external files.** The spec mandates
this. The templates are small (under 2KB each), change only when the AIDE
methodology changes, and are consumed by exactly one caller. Externalizing
them to template files would add an async read and a file-resolution step
for no reduction in coupling.

**Playbook hub structure matches the user's actual hub, content is empty.**
The template reproduces the five-section heading structure of the user's
`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md` (Task Routing, How to Use This Index,
Always Read First, Sections, Contents) because that is what the
study-playbook skill's crawling protocol expects. But it fills zero data
rows, zero section entries, and zero content links — fabricating those
would teach agents wrong conventions on the first read.

**Vault CLAUDE.md is a generic navigation guide, not a clone of the
user's global CLAUDE.md.** The user's global CLAUDE.md contains business
context, RTK instructions, environment details, and project-specific
configuration. The vault CLAUDE.md extracts only the navigation primitives
(crawling protocol, decision protocol, where-to-find-things table, brain
role) so any agent reading the vault can navigate it without prior
knowledge, regardless of which project or user owns the vault.

**No helper subfolder extraction for build functions.** The four `build*`
functions (`buildVaultStep`, `buildPlaybookHubStep`, `buildVaultClaudeMdStep`,
`buildObsidianMcpStep`) are small (under 20 lines each), share the
`exists()` utility, and are called by a single orchestrator. Extracting
each into its own subfolder would add four folders for functions that are
tightly coupled to the orchestrator's return contract. The single-helper
exception in progressive disclosure allows this when helpers are small and
the orchestrator has exactly one level of delegation. If any builder grows
complex enough to need its own tests, extract it then.
